import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Plan 0033 — the fragment tower: mlopt vocabularies as Language data","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Plan 0033 — the fragment tower: mlopt vocabularies as `Language` data","type":"plan","date":"2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/plans/0033-fragment-tower.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/plans/0033-fragment-tower.md"}'),a={name:`graphopt/project/plans/0033-fragment-tower.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="plan-0033-—-the-fragment-tower-mlopt-vocabularies-as-language-data" tabindex="-1">Plan 0033 — the fragment tower: mlopt vocabularies as <code>Language</code> data <a class="header-anchor" href="#plan-0033-—-the-fragment-tower-mlopt-vocabularies-as-language-data" aria-label="Permalink to “Plan 0033 — the fragment tower: mlopt vocabularies as Language data”">​</a></h1><p>Origin: ADR-0157 (<code>handl-lang/host</code>). The discovery stack measured its own boundary — every minted original came from vocabulary <em>growth</em>, never composition inside it. The hand-drawn generator space is the cap. This plan is the phased migration; each phase ships standalone value.</p><h2 id="phase-0-—-canonical-fragment-vocabulary" tabindex="-1">Phase 0 — canonical fragment vocabulary <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-0-—-canonical-fragment-vocabulary" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 0 — canonical fragment vocabulary”">​</a></h2><p>One fragment directory as the single source of truth for the discovery vocabulary. Today the same facts live as Python objects in five places (<code>op_def</code> registries, <code>law_to_data</code> rows, <code>_FUSION_SCHEMAS</code>, <code>handler_synth</code> family tables, <code>shipped_primitives._RECORDS</code>).</p><p>Workstream 0a — schema:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>vocab/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ops.yaml          # op name, arity, attrs, denotation-ref</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  laws.yaml         # law_to_data rows (patterns, conds, provenance)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  handlers.yaml     # minted handler bodies + domains</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  schemas.yaml      # fold-class schemas (the _FUSION_SCHEMAS table)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  prims.yaml        # shipped_primitives _RECORDS verbatim</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  families.yaml     # handler grammar family tables</span></span></code></pre></div><ul><li>Emit each from its current owner (one emitter per registry — <code>scripts/emit_vocab_fragments.py</code>), with a <code>schema:</code> version pin.</li><li><code>lawdata</code>/registries read the dir at import (or are generated from it) — the Python objects become projections, not the source.</li><li>Exit gate: <code>mlopt-handl-peer</code>&#39;s <code>transform_ids(dir)</code> loads the same dir <code>pipeline.propose</code> consumes; a fragment added by hand or by machine is visible to both engines identically.</li></ul><p>Workstream 0b — fragment emit API for the mint: <code>minted op</code> → <code>ops.yaml</code> row; <code>minted handler</code> → <code>handlers.yaml</code> row. Today these land in a Python-side <code>shipped_primitives</code> registry; after 0b they land as fragments and the registry projects them. <strong>This is the generator-fix landing</strong>: emitted vocab re-enters the next mint&#39;s search space by construction.</p><h2 id="phase-1-—-engine-handl-seat" tabindex="-1">Phase 1 — <code>engine=&quot;handl&quot;</code> seat <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-1-—-engine-handl-seat" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 1 — engine=&quot;handl&quot; seat”">​</a></h2><p><code>mlopt-handl-peer</code> graduates from spike to engine: <code>search(..., engine=&quot;handl&quot;)</code> routes law application/saturation through <code>handl-egraph</code> + <code>RewriteEngine</code>; the Python <code>EGraph</code> stays the differential oracle — every equivalence checked twice, disagreement refuses loudly. Overnight arm: <code>--engine handl</code> records parity stats beside the funnel.</p><h2 id="phase-2-—-denotations-as-models" tabindex="-1">Phase 2 — denotations as Models <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-2-—-denotations-as-models" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 2 — denotations as Models”">​</a></h2><p><code>synop_*</code> bodies + minted handlers as handl handler records over <code>host-tensor</code>. Gate: fp64 max_rel ≤ the shipped verify floor on every bound site before a Model is &quot;handl-shipped&quot;; <code>TorchSink</code> stays the delivery oracle until then.</p><h2 id="phase-3-—-mint-emits-fragments-the-principled-fix" tabindex="-1">Phase 3 — mint emits fragments (the principled fix) <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-3-—-mint-emits-fragments-the-principled-fix" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 3 — mint emits fragments (the principled fix)”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>zoo-prim</code> arm: mined shape → op row + denotation fragment merged into the ambient vocabulary → next mint sees the op as a mintable atom (depth-2 primitives).</li><li>refused-spec analysis: emits handler-<em>family</em> fragments (the index/gather family that today is hand-authored).</li><li>antiunify over oracle-failed mints: emits schema fragments (corpus-derived <code>fold:*</code>-class replacements).</li><li>Loop-closure test: run mint → emit → re-mint; a depth-2 synop must appear for the bootstrap to be real.</li></ul><h2 id="phase-4-—-play-saturation-typed-guards" tabindex="-1">Phase 4 — play + saturation + typed guards <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-4-—-play-saturation-typed-guards" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 4 — play + saturation + typed guards”">​</a></h2><p><code>Board</code> seat over fragment-application moves; <code>handl-egraph</code> becomes the saturation engine when the perf-parity gate passes; <code>check</code>/ <code>derive</code>/<code>cond</code> hooks migrate toward typecheck-nbe predicates as the dependent surface lands.</p><h2 id="hard-gates-measured-not-aspirational" tabindex="-1">Hard gates (measured, not aspirational) <a class="header-anchor" href="#hard-gates-measured-not-aspirational" aria-label="Permalink to “Hard gates (measured, not aspirational)”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>host-tensor</code> fp64 parity with <code>TorchSink</code> on the prim domains.</li><li><code>handl-egraph</code> saturation time/memory within 2× of <code>EGraph</code> on the 68-module corpus.</li><li>Fragment schema version bumps ride the <code>uv.lock</code> pin loudly — a silent schema drift is a release blocker.</li></ul><h2 id="dependencies-adjacency" tabindex="-1">Dependencies / adjacency <a class="header-anchor" href="#dependencies-adjacency" aria-label="Permalink to “Dependencies / adjacency”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>engine-typecheck-nbe</code> work (parallel session) supplies the guard-as-type surface Phase 4 wants.</li><li><code>mlopt-handl-peer</code> already proves the load-and-fire seam; Phase 0 is the only phase with no external dependency.</li><li>kernelopt follows identical phases on its own vocabulary (private fragments per ADR-0150).</li></ul>`,20)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};